cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  AI-Assisted CFD on the Cloud: A Verification Case Study of Vortex Compression
  in an Axisymmetric Cone
message: >-
  If you use this software, the recorded results, or the findings catalogue,
  please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Paoli
    given-names: Samuele
repository-code: "https://github.com/PSamuele/Navier_Stokes_CFD_Verification"
abstract: >-
  A case study in AI-assisted computational fluid dynamics on rented cloud
  compute: how far one engineer can get on a hard flow problem when most of the
  code is written with an AI assistant and the runs are done on AWS, and what it
  takes to trust the result. The test case is a vortex ring in an axisymmetric
  domain that narrows to a 6-degree cone, solved with the 3D axisymmetric
  incompressible Navier-Stokes equations in FEniCSx. On the two finer of three
  grids the computation stays consistent to T = 0.55 (kinetic energy decreases
  in every interval, circulation obeys its maximum principle to
  within 0.03 %). The vortex ring
  never reaches the narrowing tip, so the compression mechanism the setup was
  designed around is not tested, and no claim about singularities is made.
  Accompanying the result is a catalogue of five silent failure modes found in two earlier
  attempts by the same author - a mesh size field that degenerated to a constant,
  a vorticity diagnostic amplifying round-off by 1e14 on the symmetry axis, a
  breakdown threshold triggered by differentiated sampling noise, a stale
  momentum operator, and a projection step solved without boundary conditions -
  none of which raised an error, together with the regression tests that detect
  each. The repository also documents a grid convergence study whose own
  diagnostics reveal that the mesh was refined according to geometric intuition
  rather than the flow, leaving the wall boundary layer where the extreme
  vorticity actually lives resolved by under one element.
keywords:
  - computational fluid dynamics
  - Navier-Stokes equations
  - finite-time singularity
  - AI-assisted development
  - cloud computing
  - verification and validation
  - grid convergence index
  - axisymmetric flow
  - FEniCSx
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
